WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:01.920
- [Narrator] Signs of the drought

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.410
in the Western United
States are everywhere.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.560
From the dry riverbeds

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.620
to the depleted reservoirs,
canals and fallow fields.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.820
And Western farmers are hurting.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:11.840
- This is a fallow field

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.790
where there's not enough water to plant.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.860
No water, so that's the rice dying.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.120
- [Narrator] Right now, the
US is in year two of a drought

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.420
that encompasses almost the
entirety of the American west.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.840
And though dry periods are part

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.940
of a natural water cycle in the west,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.850
climate scientists are noticing changes

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:29.920
that are making it easier

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.200
for the region to fall into drought.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.300
This is causing farmers
to go to greater lengths

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.210
to access their water, which
can create a ripple effect

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.133
in neighboring rural communities.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.710
Water moves in the west in the same cycle

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.560
as the rest of the world.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.600
First, precipitation falls,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.170
then the water seeps into the ground

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.160
or becomes runoff that flows
into rivers and streams.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.090
It's either sapped up by plants or humans

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.440
or evaporates back into the atmosphere,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.010
starting the process all over again.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.020
But what's unique about the west is where

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.500
and how the precipitation falls.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.780
Experts say it often comes in
the form of a few big storms,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:07.750
traversing the region,

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.790
and falling a snow in the mountains,

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.990
like in the Rockies or
California, Sierra and Nevada.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.120
The snow pack it creates
serves as a natural storage,

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.130
saving water until the spring
and summer when it thaws,

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:20.660
feeding the rivers and reservoirs

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.450
that serve lower lying farms and cities.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.270
- Some of these extreme
rain events can really make

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:28.310
or break drought conditions
for a given winter.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.020
- [Narrator] Climate scientist
Samantha Stevenson says,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.550
some causes of a drought can occur

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.550
at two points in the water cycle.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:36.170
The first is more precipitation

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.330
falling as rain instead of snow.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.900
- If you're getting the
same amount of moisture

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.010
added to the land surface,

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:44.170
but more of that is
ending up falling as rain,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.560
then it's going to just run
off into the river very quickly

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.850
and be further downstream,
or maybe even out of the area

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.420
where you need it by the
time summer comes around.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.420
- [Narrator] Second reason
is increased evaporation

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.100
from the land, rivers and lakes,

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.560
making everything very dry.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.210
- We've all seen this
during dry summers, right?

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.880
You water your lawn and then it disappears

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:04.830
within a couple of hours.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.580
And it's the same thing,
but on a larger scale.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.990
- [Narrator] Experts
say higher temperatures

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.300
have led to both occurring this year,

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.670
resulting in the current drought

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.460
being so severe and so widespread.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.870
They cautioned him might be a sign

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.900
of similar droughts to come.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.890
- What we can say is that we expect

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.100
that these temperature driven trends

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.900
are going to cause droughts
to in general, become larger.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.128
- [Narrator] Right now, the drought

00:02:29.128 --> 00:02:30.230
is very large and very dry.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:31.760
Snow pack levels in California

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.440
have dropped to 4% of normal this year.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.290
And many of the west's largest reservoirs

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.890
have sunk below half of their capacities.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.010
The effects are most acutely felt by farms

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.280
and nearby communities, where over 90%

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.900
of the water goes in many Western states.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.460
California accounts for a quarter

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.630
of all food grown in the US,

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.070
including 40% of fruits,
nuts and other table foods.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.340
That's because they can thrive

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.510
in the region semi-arid climate.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.080
The water is put to good
use says Doug Parker,

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.090
the director of the California Institute

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:02.790
for water resources.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.570
- So the dry season is sort of a curse

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.590
because we don't have
water during that season,

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.150
but then again, it's a blessing

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.740
'cause it's the only way
we could actually grow

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.120
some of those kinds of crops.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.500
- [Narrator] Jamie
Traynham and her husband

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.330
manage over 1200 acres of
farmland in Colusa County.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:21.110
- My husband and I have
been farming for 27 years.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.955
We farm about 1100 acres of rice,

00:03:22.955 --> 00:03:27.480
right around 200 acres of
walnuts, 60 acres of row crop.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.940
And then we run 250 head of mother cows.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.470
We ended up having to follow
right around 500 acres of rice.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.850
We didn't have much
choice, there's nothing,

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.580
it's just dirt and some, you
know, dry weeds basically.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.240
If there's not water for
it, you can't grow it.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.430
- [Narrator] Most of Traynham's water

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.450
originates 120 miles away in Shasta lake,

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:49.910
and makes its way to her farm

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.630
via a series of canals and pipelines.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.540
But the US Bureau of Reclamation,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.730
which manages much of this infrastructure

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.870
and provides one fifth of Western farmers

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.810
with your irrigation water
has cut the water allocations

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.030
for many to zero this year.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.270
- When there's less water,
you spend 10 times more

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:10.490
of your own time dealing with paperwork,

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:14.730
calculations and managing
what little water you have.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.130
It's very, very stressful
and nerve wracking

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.080
because it's like
putting a puzzle together

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:23.500
when you don't even know
what all the pieces are.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.610
- [Narrator] During
the 2014, 2015 drought,

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.750
total farm related losses in California

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:32.120
totaled $5 billion and 20,000
farm hands lost their jobs.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.860
- Imagine if you have
to basically cut back

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.800
20% of your acreage, that's
like taking a 20% pay cut

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.010
to somebody who works
in a regular job, right?

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.820
So it is painful for those farmers.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:46.000
- [Narrator] Experts say the
region's agricultural sector

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.930
is resilient and it's too early to tell

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.140
what effects this drought will have

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:50.720
on food supplies and prices.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.670
With limited options, Traynham has turned

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.910
to pumping groundwater to make ends meet.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:58.010
- Right now in the rice that we do have,

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.310
we really don't have any
other source of water

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:01.450
but our well water.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.520
If you don't have a well,

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.000
and you're relying on surface water,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.440
years like this prove the point

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.810
that you better rethink
what you're planting

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.490
or you better have a backup plan.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:13.323
- [Narrator] But groundwater

00:05:13.323 --> 00:05:15.660
isn't as easily replenished
as surface water.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.770
And there are concerns
that California farms

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.010
run the risk of overdrawing,
causing wells to run dry.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:22.150
- We don't have a good system

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.290
where we regulate that groundwater.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.270
And so we tend to have a
system where we draw more

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.610
and we put back less, and
as anybody knows, you know,

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.060
you can't continually take
money out of your bank account

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.650
if you never put something back in.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.730
- [Narrator] Nearby rural
communities also rely

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:38.620
on this groundwater for their homes.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.460
During the last drought, hundreds
of California households,

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.600
many in the central valley
reported water shortages.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.040
There are other consequences as well.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.100
Over pumping has been
linked to increased levels

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.970
of arsenic in parts of the
central Valley's groundwater.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.870
It also can cause changes
in land subsidence,

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.450
meaning the ground can sink,

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.840
potentially roads, homes and bridges.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.800
- I'm concerned about
the use of the well water

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.530
just like everybody is, nobody
wants their well to go dry.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.330
Nobody wants subsidence.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.700
We suffer and feel it first,

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.720
but that doesn't make it
any less of a struggle

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.907
to somebody living in a city

00:06:16.907 --> 00:06:20.070
where their well goes dry and
they have no drinking water.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:20.903
- [Narrator] During the last drought,

00:06:20.903 --> 00:06:24.460
California passed laws to
better manage groundwater usage.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.610
- We're in the early parts of figuring out

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.600
how to do that management of it.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.530
We really don't have all
those mechanisms in place yet.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.500
So I expect us to overuse groundwater

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.680
by quite a bit during this drought.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.270
- [Narrator] Experts expect
the drought will continue

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:39.420
to worsen into the summer and early fall,

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.060
as temperatures rise even more,

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:43.110
which could cause a tighter
squeeze on resources

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:44.660
and tougher decisions to be made

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.909
about how the west gets its water.

00:06:46.909 --> 00:06:49.492
(gentle music)

